A||||5||Brian84SE||Bleeding the cooling system||||1||
Z||000000||Brian84SE||02-27-2000||06:39 PM||||I went to bleed the cooling system and I removed the thermostat cap, removed the thermostat.  The water level immediately dropped down.  The level in the expansion tank rose!  Is this supposed to do this?  I should add I just put on a new radiator and thermostat cap.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||fiero56||02-27-2000||07:17 PM||||Make sure your radiator cap is not of the ventilated type. I bought a Stant cap, and their book recommended the ventilated cap, the same thing happened to me. I went back out, boght a 16lb release non-ventilated, and haven't had the problem since.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Brian84SE||02-27-2000||08:45 PM||||Fiero 56:  That's exactly what I got! A stant  vented one.  What is the difference and how does it affect cooling?  What do I ask for at the parts store?<BR>Thanx so much!!!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ed||02-27-2000||11:52 PM||||You folks weren't nose down on a steep hill, were you? ||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero56||02-28-2000||08:26 AM||||The problem ocurrs because the on the Fiero's cooling system, the radiator cap is not in a higher position to the coolant. The vented cap will allow the coolant out until teh coolant level reaches the level of the cap. If you look at the underside of most all radiator caps, there is a little brass disk held on by some sort of elasti string. On the non-vented ones this brass disk is pulled tight against the cap, making an airtight seal. On the vented ones it hangs loose, allowing air and coolant out.<BR>When you go back, just get a regular non-vented 16lb release cap. That should take care of the problem.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Brian84SE||02-28-2000||07:18 PM||||I got one, and it worked!  They tried to sell me a vented one. But I told em!<P>thanks again,<BR>Brian<BR>||||reg||1
